[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"CloudWatch: Monitoring and Observability in AWS"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Amazon CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service built for DevOps engineers, developers, site reliability engineers (SREs), and IT managers. CloudWatch provides you with data and actionable insights to monitor your applications, respond to system-wide performance changes, optimize resource utilization, and gain a unified view of operational health."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Features of CloudWatch"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Metrics: Collect metrics from AWS services, applications, and custom sources. Metrics are time-ordered sets of data points that are published to CloudWatch."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Logs: Centralize logs from your applications and AWS services. CloudWatch Logs enables you to monitor, store, and access your log files."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Alarms: Set alarms that trigger actions when metrics cross defined thresholds. Alarms can notify you via SNS, trigger Auto Scaling actions, or perform other automated tasks."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Events: React to changes in your AWS environment. CloudWatch Events (now EventBridge) delivers a near real-time stream of system events that describe changes in your AWS resources."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dashboards: Create custom dashboards to visualize metrics and logs. Dashboards provide a unified view of your operational data."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Synthetics: Monitor your application endpoints using configurable scripts. CloudWatch Synthetics allows you to create canaries that check the availability and performance of your endpoints."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up CloudWatch Monitoring"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Most AWS services automatically send metrics to CloudWatch. For example, EC2 instances send CPU utilization, network I/O, and disk I/O metrics. You can also install the CloudWatch agent on your EC2 instances or on-premises servers to collect additional metrics and logs."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"# Example: Installing the CloudWatch agent on an EC2 instance (Linux)
sudo yum update -y
sudo yum install -y amazon-cloudwatch-agent
"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"After installing the agent, you need to configure it to specify which metrics and logs to collect. You can create a configuration file in JSON format."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"# Example: CloudWatch agent configuration file (config.json)
{
  "agent": {
    "metrics_collected": {
      "cpu": {
        "measurement": [
          "cpu_usage_idle",
          "cpu_usage_iowait",
          "cpu_usage_system",
          "cpu_usage_user"
        ],
        "metrics_collection_interval": 60,
        "totalcpu": false
      },
      "disk": {
        "measurement": [
          "disk_free",
          "disk_total",
          "disk_used",
          "disk_used_percent"
        ],
        "metrics_collection_interval": 60,
        "resources": [
          "*"
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Then, start the CloudWatch agent using the configuration file:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl -a fetch-config -m ec2 -c file:/opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/etc/config.json -s"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating CloudWatch Alarms"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"CloudWatch alarms allow you to automatically react to changes in your AWS resources. You can create alarms based on metrics and specify thresholds that trigger actions when crossed."},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, you can create an alarm that sends an SNS notification when the CPU utilization of an EC2 instance exceeds 80%."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Steps to create an alarm in the AWS Management Console:"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Navigate to the CloudWatch console."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Select 'Alarms' in the left navigation pane."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Click 'Create alarm'."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Specify the metric you want to monitor (e.g., CPUUtilization for an EC2 instance)."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Define the threshold and evaluation period."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Configure the actions to be taken when the alarm state changes (e.g., send an SNS notification)."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Name and create the alarm."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"CloudWatch Logs Insights"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"CloudWatch Logs Insights enables you to interactively search and analyze your log data in CloudWatch Logs. You can use a powerful query language to find specific events, identify trends, and troubleshoot issues."},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":"# Example: CloudWatch Logs Insights query to find error events
fields @timestamp, @message
| filter @message like /error/
| sort @timestamp desc
| limit 20"},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"CloudWatch Dashboards"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"CloudWatch dashboards provide a customizable view of your metrics and logs. You can create dashboards to monitor the health and performance of your applications and infrastructure."},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can add widgets to your dashboard to display metrics, logs, and alarms. You can also customize the layout and appearance of your dashboard to suit your needs."},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"CloudWatch Synthetics"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"CloudWatch Synthetics allows you to create canaries that monitor your application endpoints. Canaries are configurable scripts that check the availability and performance of your endpoints."},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use canaries to monitor your APIs, websites, and other endpoints. Synthetics canaries help you detect issues before your customers do."},{"order":37,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for CloudWatch Monitoring"},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor key metrics: Focus on monitoring the metrics that are most important to your application's performance and availability."},{"order":39,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Set appropriate alarm thresholds: Configure alarms with thresholds that are sensitive enough to detect issues early, but not so sensitive that they generate false positives."},{"order":40,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use CloudWatch Logs Insights to analyze logs: Leverage CloudWatch Logs Insights to quickly identify and troubleshoot issues."},{"order":41,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create custom dashboards: Build dashboards that provide a unified view of your operational data."},{"order":42,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Automate remediation: Use CloudWatch Events and Lambda functions to automate remediation tasks."},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"By following these best practices, you can use CloudWatch to effectively monitor your applications and infrastructure in AWS."}]
